\date{2026-03-13T20:51:52Z}
\tag{discrete}

\import{discrete-macros}

\taxon{Example}
\p{Let #{a_0, a_1, \dots} be a sequence satisfying the recurrence ##{a_n = 2a_{n-1} + 1} with #{a_0 = 0}.}

\p{We have #{a_1 = 2a_0 + 1 = 1}; then #{a_2 = 2 \cdot 1 + 1 = 3}; then #{a_3 = 7}; and so on. The sequence starts ##{0, 1, 3, 7, 15, 31, 63, \dots}}

\p{Looking at this we notice that every term of the sequence seems to be one less than a power of two, so we conjecture the closed form ##{a_n = 2^n - 1.} First, let’s check that this works for #{a_0}, which it does: #{2^0 - 1 = 1 - 1 = 0}. Next, let’s see if #{a_n} satisfies the recurrence when defined in this way. We can do this by substituting our conjectured closed form for #{a_n} into the recurrence and seeing if the two sides are equal: ##{2a_{n-1} + 1 = 2(2^{n-1} - 1) + 1 = 2^n - 2 + 1 = 2^n - 1 = a_n.} Yay! This is in fact a valid proof that this closed form and recurrence define the same sequence.}
